\eqn{X + Z} on the \strong{pooled} sample. Two axes are reported, both as
incremental McFadden \eqn{R^2}, so that they share one scale:
\deqn{\Delta R^2 = \frac{D(X) - D(X + Z)}{D_0},}
the drop in deviance from adding \eqn{Z}, divided by the null deviance of
the same response on the same rows.
\itemize{
\item \strong{relevance}: \eqn{\Delta R^2} of \eqn{Z} in the outcome model;
\item \strong{exclusion}: \eqn{\Delta R^2} of \eqn{Z} in the response model, i.e.
the size of the exclusion violation;
\item \strong{ratio}: relevance / exclusion. Above one, \eqn{Z} predicts the outcome
better than it predicts selection.
